the u.s. and Cuba have had one of the  world's strangest relationships think of  it a Caribbean island nation of just 11  million people locked in a 50-year  standoff with the superpower but now  with the re-establishment of ties and  easing of a trade embargo can these once  bitter enemies become good neighbors so  here's how we got to the current  situation the u.s. severed relations  with Cuba in 1961 after its leader Fidel  Castro allied Cuba with the Soviet Union  at the height of the Cold War when the  u.s. failed to remove Castro with the  Bay of Pigs invasion and imposed a trade  embargo and that's how things stood for  about a half a century but then  relations between the two sides formally  resumed in July 2015 today I can  announce that the United States has  agreed to formally re-establish  diplomatic relations with the Republic  of Cuba the diplomatic thaw was made  possible by swap of imprisoned spies and  Cuba's release of a jailed American  contractor as part of the deal with the  u.s. Cuba's government promised to  expand some freedoms and it released 53  political prisoners then afterwards it  stepped up arrests of dissidents on the  other side Barack Obama used executive  authority to make it easier for US  citizens to travel to Cuba and in May  2015 the u.s. removed Cuba from its list  of state sponsors of terrorism the u.s.  terror watch lists the US military base  Gitmo is not part of the deal now here's  the argument Obama says he wants the US  trade embargo on Cuba fully lifted but  that would require an act of Congress  closer us ties could also help  marginalize left-leaning Cuban allies  like Venezuela but opponents of  normalization say Obama has only thrown  a lifeline to a dying dictatorship the  bottom line without support from  Congress  Obama's changes could be undone by the  next president
 

